# Every fault that must exist.
#
# Without this file, deleting a fault directory is a silent way to make the
# catalogue pass: fewer entries, all green, nothing to see. That is the same
# move as deleting a failing test, one level up, and it is cheaper because a
# fault has no obvious owner and its absence looks like it was never there.
#
# run-fault-catalogue.sh requires every name here to exist, and every
# directory present to be named here. It also requires every method of the
# catalogue class to be claimed by a directory, so the code and this list
# cannot drift apart either. Removing a fault stays allowed — a defect can
# genuinely stop being expressible, or turn out to be caught by unit tests
# already — but it takes an edit to this file, which lands in the diff where
# a reviewer will see the entry and its why.md disappearing together.
#
# Removed when the catalogue moved from patches into code:
#   test-method-lock-lifted — lifting the base-branch test-method lock fails
#   five methods of TestMethodProtectionTest and two of
#   GitManagedJobDegradedStatusTest, so the unit tests already watch it fail.
#
# One name per line. Comments and blank lines ignored.

control-no-defect
required-mcp-server-kills-job
dropped-work-reported-as-success
all-changes-dropped-not-reported
missing-commit-message-drops-work
reverted-work-not-reported
tampering-restart-skipped
